Progress status for this period: stalling. This was computed from strengthIndex by a fixed rule, so treat it as settled and use progressiveOverload.notes to explain it with the specific numbers. The rule: regressing if any muscle group's index dropped 3+ points, otherwise on_track if any rose 3+ points, otherwise stalling; changes are measured against previousIndex, or against 100 (the user's first recorded level) when previousIndex is null.

Analyze this data and submit a structured recommendation using the submit_training_recommendation tool. Base all quantitative judgments strictly on the numbers provided above — do not invent or assume any data not present here. Use the user's age, sex, and BMI, if provided in userContext, to calibrate what counts as reasonable training volume and intensity for their profile. Use strengthIndex as your primary evidence for progressive overload. Use volumeSplit and routineAdherence together to judge balance and consistency. Factor in the user's long-term goal and this period's note (if provided) when shaping your advice and action items. Only make physiological claims that are well established — for example, do not claim that training one muscle group raises hormone levels enough to drive muscle growth elsewhere. If totalSets is 0, note that no training was logged this period rather than speculating why.

Respond in English. Every text field you submit must be plain prose only — no XML tags, no markdown formatting, no stray closing tags of any kind. The headline must stand completely on its own — write it as if it's the only sentence the user will ever read.

{
  "headline": "Strength gains have stalled this week, so it's time to shake things up.",
  "summary": "You put in a solid week of work, logging 28 sets and over 10,700 kg of total tonnage, which is a respectable output for a 28-year-old male at your bodyweight and training level. The effort is clearly there. However, the strength index tells us that the needle hasn't moved meaningfully across your main muscle groups this period, which means your body has adapted to the current stimulus and needs a new challenge to keep progressing toward your long-term strength goal. The good news is that stalling is a completely normal part of strength development, and a few targeted adjustments should get things moving again.",
  "progressiveOverload": {
    "notes": "The strength index shows minimal movement across all muscle groups this period. Chest edged up one point from 104 to 105, and shoulders rose one point from 101 to 102, which are small positive signs but well below the three-point threshold needed to classify as on-track progress. Back held flat at 103, biceps held flat at 101, and triceps held flat at 104. Most notably, legs actually dropped one point from 108 to 107, signaling a slight regression on that movement pattern. None of these changes are severe, but collectively they confirm a stalling trend. Per your long-term goal of increasing strength, this is the key signal to act on: the current loading scheme is no longer driving adaptation, and a deliberate change in stimulus, whether through increased load, adjusted rep ranges, or varied exercise selection, is needed to restart progress.",
